## Working with Multiple Shared Borrows

Shared borrows (`&T`) are the foundation of safe concurrent read access. Multiple `&T` references can coexist freely.

### Multiple &T in Practice

```xiom
fn analyze(a: &Vec[Int], b: &Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  return a.len() + b.len();
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let data = Vec[Int]();
  data.push(1); data.push(2);

  let r1 = &data;
  let r2 = &data;
  let r3 = &data;
  // All three borrows are valid simultaneously
  return r1.len() + r2.len() + r3.len();
}
```

### &T Through Method Calls

```xiom
pub type Counter = { value: Int; }

fn Counter.value(self: &Counter) -> Int {
  return self.value;
}

fn Counter.compare(self: &Counter, other: &Counter) -> Bool {
  return self.value == other.value;  // can read both
}

let c1 = Counter{ value: 5 };
let c2 = Counter{ value: 5 };
let same = c1.compare(&c2);  // two shared borrows
```

### &T and Mutability

```xiom
var data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(1);

// You can shared-borrow a var variable
let r = &data;
let len = r.len();
// But you can't mutable-borrow while shared borrow exists
// let m = &mut data;  // ERROR

// After r's last use, mutable borrow is OK
let m = &mut data;
m.push(2);
```

### &T is Read-Only

```xiom
let data = Vec[Int]();
data.push(42);
let r = &data;
// r.push(99);  // ERROR: cannot modify through &T
```

### &T Common Use Cases

- Function parameters that only read: `fn len(v: &Vec[Int]) -> Int`
- Comparing values: `fn eq(a: &Point, b: &Point) -> Bool`
- Calculating derived values: `fn area(r: &Rect) -> Float64`

